---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'how-to-clean-phone-speaker-dust')!;
const url = `https://fixthespeaker.com/guides/${guide.slug}`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: guide.title,
  description: guide.description,
  datePublished: guide.published,
  dateModified: guide.updated,
  mainEntityOfPage: url,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Dev', url: 'https://fixthespeaker.com/about' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://fixthespeaker.com/#organization' },
};
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${guide.title} | Fix The Speaker`}
  description={guide.description}
  heading={guide.title}
  eyebrow="Guide"
  updated={guide.updated}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> use a small, soft, clean and dry brush, and brush lightly across the grille.
    Both Apple and Samsung recommend a soft brush, and Samsung specifically advises against air guns. Skip pins,
    toothpicks, liquids and compressed air.
  </p>

  <h2>Why phone speakers get clogged</h2>
  <p>
    Phone speakers sit behind rows of tiny holes or a slot covered with fine mesh. Pockets and bags are full of lint,
    and sand, makeup and dust stick to that mesh. Over months it builds up into a grey layer that you can often see
    with a torch. Less open mesh means less sound gets out, so the speaker sounds quieter or muffled.
  </p>
  <p>
    The earpiece at the top of the phone collects grime too, mostly skin oil and makeup from calls. If callers sound
    faint but music from the bottom speaker is fine, that top grille is the one to clean.
  </p>

  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A small soft-bristled brush that is clean and dry.</strong> A new soft toothbrush, a clean makeup or
      eyeshadow brush, or a small paintbrush all work. Apple's own advice for a blocked speaker opening is exactly
      this: a small, soft-bristled brush that is clean and dry.
    </li>
    <li><strong>A lint-free cloth</strong> for wiping the outside of the phone afterwards.</li>
    <li><strong>A torch</strong> (another phone works) so you can see the grille.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Turn the phone off and take off the case.</strong> Cases often hide a ring of lint right at the
      speaker opening.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Look first.</strong> Shine the torch across the grille at an angle. Dust and lint show up as grey
      patches over the holes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Brush with the grille facing down.</strong> Hold the phone so the speaker points at the floor and brush
      lightly across the holes, side to side. Facing down lets loosened dust fall out instead of into the phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Go gently and take your time.</strong> Samsung's guidance is to brush lightly with a brush that will not
      scratch. Pressing hard can push dirt through the mesh.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tap out what is left.</strong> Tap the edge of the phone against your palm, speaker side down, then
      wipe the outside with the cloth.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Test the sound.</strong> Turn the phone on and play something familiar at medium volume. If one side
      still sounds weaker, brush that grille again.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Can a sound help?</h2>
  <p>
    A little. Playing a high-pitched sweep makes the speaker mesh vibrate, which can loosen light dust so the brush
    lifts it more easily. Our <a href="/">dust cleaner mode</a> plays this kind of sweep. Be realistic, though: a
    sound will not pull out packed lint or dried grime. The brush does most of the work.
  </p>

  <h2>What not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pins, needles and toothpicks.</strong> They can tear the mesh or push dirt deeper, and a torn mesh lets
      in more dust and water.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Compressed air.</strong> Samsung warns that strong air pressure forced into the speaker, receiver or
      microphone holes can damage parts inside.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Liquids, including alcohol and water.</strong> Liquid carries the dirt deeper and can wet the
      speaker itself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sticky putty or tape.</strong> Some people press cleaning putty into the grille. It can leave residue
      in the holes that is harder to remove than the original dust.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When cleaning is not the answer</h2>
  <p>
    If the sound is muffled but the grille looks clean, check a few other things before you blame dust:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Is a case or screen protector covering part of the speaker opening?</li>
    <li>Is the phone connected to Bluetooth headphones or a car without you noticing?</li>
    <li>Did the phone get wet recently? Water in the grille sounds a lot like dust. See our guide on <a href="/guides/muffled-phone-speaker-water-dust-or-damage">telling water, dust and damage apart</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If the speaker crackles, buzzes, or makes no sound at all after a careful clean, the speaker itself may be
    damaged. Apple's troubleshooting page says that in that case your speaker might need service, and the same
    applies to any Android phone. A repair shop can replace a speaker; no cleaning method can fix a torn one.
  </p>

